---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import StatusBadge from "../components/StatusBadge.astro";
import { getCollection } from "astro:content";
import {
  studies, studyPath, publishedPosts, postPath, timeline, people, fmtDate, fmtRange, evidenceOf,
  STATUS_LABEL, STATUS_HELP, SPLIT_LABEL,
} from "../lib/data";

const ws = (await getCollection("workstreams")).sort((a, b) => a.data.id.localeCompare(b.data.id));
const all = await studies();
const posts = (await publishedPosts()).slice(0, 3);

// Verdict breakdown, in the order a reader weighs them.
const ORDER = ["closed-positive", "closed-negative", "closed-inconclusive", "running", "planned", "abandoned"];
const tally = ORDER.map((st) => ({ st, n: all.filter((s) => s.data.status === st).length })).filter((t) => t.n > 0);
const closedTally = tally.filter((t) => t.st.startsWith("closed"));
const openTally = tally.filter((t) => !t.st.startsWith("closed"));
const finished = closedTally.reduce((n, t) => n + t.n, 0);
// The tally reads as one sentence: "5 studies finished: 1 Confirmed, 3 Did not hold and 1 Inconclusive; 1 In progress."
// Separators are separate text parts so JSX whitespace never lands before a comma.
const tallyParts: ({ st: string; n: number; text?: undefined } | { st?: undefined; n?: undefined; text: string })[] = [];
closedTally.forEach((t, i) => {
  if (i > 0) tallyParts.push({ text: i === closedTally.length - 1 ? " and " : ", " });
  tallyParts.push(t);
});
openTally.forEach((t, i) => {
  tallyParts.push({ text: i === 0 ? "; " : ", " });
  tallyParts.push(t);
});
tallyParts.push({ text: "." });
const supervisor = people().find((p) => p.role === "supervisor")?.name ?? "our supervisor";
const w1 = all.filter((s) => s.data.workstream === "W1");
const w1Finished = w1.filter((s) => s.data.status.startsWith("closed")).length;
const w1Negative = w1.filter((s) => s.data.status === "closed-negative").length;
const lastEntry = new Date(Math.max(...all.map((s) => (s.data.closed ?? s.data.started).getTime())));

// Workstreams without studies say what they are and when they are scheduled, from timeline.yaml.
const phases = timeline();
const asDate = (v: unknown) => new Date(v as string);
const phase = (id: string) => phases.find((p) => p.id === id);
const task = (name: string) => phases.flatMap((p) => p.tasks).find((t) => t.name === name);
const span = (x?: { start: unknown; end: unknown }) => (x ? fmtRange(asDate(x.start), asDate(x.end)) : "");
const plans: Record<string, { label: string; when: string; text: string; link?: { href: string; label: string } }> = {
  W0: { label: "", when: span(phase("p-foundations")), text: "Complete: the literature review, scope and method, and the proposal. It ran no experiments, so it has no studies.", link: { href: "/proposal/", label: "Read the proposal" } },
  W3: { label: "Planned", when: span(task("Hard freezing with pluggable scores")), text: "Starts once W1 and W2 have settled whether ASF-S holds up." },
  W4: { label: "Planned", when: span(task("Metaplastic soft consolidation")), text: "Starts once W1 and W2 have settled whether ASF-S holds up." },
  W5: { label: "Planned", when: span(phase("p-final")), text: "Final runs with one look at the test set, the thesis, and the poster." },
};

// One real record, annotated, shows how every study is kept.
const example = all.find((s) => s.data.id === "cifar10-repro") ?? all.find((s) => s.data.status.startsWith("closed"));
const ex = example?.data;
const kr = ex?.key_results?.[0];
const num = (v: number) => v.toFixed(2).replace(/^-/, "−");
---
<Base title="NeuroPulse">
  <div class="container">
    <section class="opening" aria-labelledby="intro-title">
    <div class="intro-head">
      <h1 id="intro-title">Can a network learn new tasks without forgetting, by keeping what it needs to function?</h1>
      <p class="byline">
        Four final-year students, Department of Computer Science and Engineering, University of Moratuwa.
        Supervised by {supervisor}. <a href="/team/">Team</a>
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="intro">
      <p class="lede">
        We are testing an idea from neuroscience-inspired pruning: when a network learns tasks one after another, protect the parts that
        matter most for how it <em>functions</em>, not just its largest weights.
      </p>
      <p class="tally">
        <span><strong>{finished} {finished === 1 ? "study" : "studies"} finished:</strong> {tallyParts.map((p) =>
          p.st ? <>{`${p.n} `}<span class={`status status-${p.st}`}>{STATUS_LABEL[p.st]}</span></> : p.text
        )}</span>
        <span class="tally-date">Last entry {fmtDate(lastEntry)}</span>
      </p>
      <div class="intro-actions">
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href="#studies">Go to the study table</a>
        <a class="text-link" href="/research/">How the project fits together</a>
      </div>
    </div>

    <aside class="plan-note" aria-labelledby="plan-title">
      <h2 id="plan-title">Why the plan changed</h2>
      <p>
        Our August 2026 proposal set out two continual learning methods built on the ASF-S pruning method (W3 and W4). Before building on
        ASF-S, we needed to know whether it works as its paper reports, so reproducing it (W1) and testing its central idea fairly (W2) came first.
        So far, {w1Negative} of the {w1Finished} finished W1 studies did not hold.
        <a href="/research/w0/">Read the full background</a>.
      </p>
    </aside>
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="studies" aria-labelledby="studies-title">
      <div class="section-head">
        <h2 id="studies-title">Studies by workstream</h2>
        <p class="muted">Each workstream is one thread of the project. Each study under it asks one question and records the answer.</p>
      </div>
      <dl class="legend" aria-label="What each status means">
        {ORDER.map((st) => (
          <div><dt><span class={`status status-${st}`}>{STATUS_LABEL[st]}</span></dt><dd>{STATUS_HELP[st]}</dd></div>
        ))}
      </dl>
      <p class="legend-note">
        <strong>Post hoc</strong> means the success criterion was written after results existed; <strong>pre-registered</strong> means before.
        Seeds count the independent training runs behind a number. Terms such as ASF-S, AFC and PGI are defined in the <a href="/glossary/">glossary</a>.
      </p>
      <table class="board">
        <thead>
          <tr><th class="col-status" scope="col">Status</th><th scope="col">Study</th><th class="col-evidence" scope="col">Evidence</th><th class="col-dates" scope="col">Dates</th></tr>
        </thead>
        {ws.map((w) => {
          const mine = all.filter((s) => s.data.workstream === w.data.id).sort((a, b) => a.data.started.getTime() - b.data.started.getTime());
          const plan = plans[w.data.id];
          return (
            <tbody>
              <tr class="ws-row">
                <th colspan="4" scope="colgroup">
                  <div class="ws-head">
                    <span class="ws-tag">{w.data.id}</span>
                    <h3><a href={`/research/${w.data.id.toLowerCase()}/`}>{w.data.title}</a></h3>
                  </div>
                  <span class="ws-question">{w.data.tagline}</span>
                </th>
              </tr>
              {mine.map((s) => (
                <tr class="study-row">
                  <td class="label-cell"><StatusBadge status={s.data.status} /></td>
                  <td>
                    <a class="study-link" href={studyPath(s)}>{s.data.title}</a>
                    <span class="owner">{s.data.owner.join(", ")}</span>
                  </td>
                  <td class="evidence">{evidenceOf(s.data)}</td>
                  <td class="dates">{fmtRange(s.data.started, s.data.closed)}</td>
                </tr>
              ))}
              {mine.length === 0 && plan && (
                <tr class="plan-row">
                  <td class="label-cell">{plan.label && <span class="status status-planned">{plan.label}</span>}</td>
                  <td colspan="2">{plan.text}{plan.link && <> <a href={plan.link.href}>{plan.link.label}</a>.</>}</td>
                  <td class="dates">{plan.when}</td>
                </tr>
              )}
              {mine.length === 0 && !plan && (
                <tr class="plan-row"><td class="label-cell"><span class="status status-planned">Planned</span></td><td colspan="3">No studies yet.</td></tr>
              )}
            </tbody>
          );
        })}
      </table>
    </section>

    {ex && example && (
      <section class="section" aria-labelledby="record-title">
        <div class="section-head">
          <h2 id="record-title">How to read a study</h2>
          <p class="muted">Every study page follows the same record. Here is a real one, the CIFAR-10 reproduction, with the rules it follows.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="record-wrap">
          <dl class="record">
            <div><dt>Question<sup>1</sup></dt><dd>{ex.question}</dd></div>
            <div>
              <dt>Criterion<sup>1</sup></dt>
              <dd>
                {ex.criterion === "pre_registered" ? "Pre-registered: written down before any results existed." : "Post hoc: written or revised after results existed, and labelled as such."}
                <span class="muted">The full success criterion is on the study page.</span>
              </dd>
            </div>
            {kr && (
              <div>
                <dt>Headline number<sup>2</sup></dt>
                <dd class="record-result">
                  <strong>{num(kr.value)}{kr.std !== undefined && ` ± ${kr.std.toFixed(2)}`}</strong> {kr.unit}
                  {kr.compare && <> against <strong>{num(kr.compare.value)}</strong> ({kr.compare.label})</>}
                  <span class="muted">{kr.label}. Measured on {SPLIT_LABEL[kr.split]}, {kr.n_seeds} {kr.n_seeds === 1 ? "seed" : "seeds"}.</span>
                </dd>
              </div>
            )}
            {ex.verdict && <div><dt>Verdict<sup>3</sup></dt><dd><StatusBadge status={ex.status} /> <span class="muted">{ex.verdict}</span></dd></div>}
            <div><dt>Code and records<sup>2</sup></dt><dd><a href={ex.code_url}>Code, configuration and lab log on GitHub</a>. <a href={studyPath(example)}>Open the full study page</a>.</dd></div>
          </dl>
          <ol class="principles">
            <li><h3>One question per study</h3><p>Every experiment belongs to a study with a question, a hypothesis and a success criterion, and we say whether that criterion came before or after the results.</p></li>
            <li><h3>Every number has a source</h3><p>Each result says whether it comes from validation or test data and how many seeds it averages. Each run records its code version and settings.</p></li>
            <li><h3>Negative results count</h3><p>When something does not reproduce, we publish it with the same care, and separate what we ruled out from what we only suspect.</p></li>
          </ol>
        </div>
        <p class="muted small" style="margin-top:20px">
          The rules are in our <a href="https://github.com/NeuroPulse-Co/.github/blob/main/standards/RECORDS.md">record standard</a>,
          and the code for every study is on <a href="https://github.com/NeuroPulse-Co">GitHub</a>.
        </p>
      </section>
    )}

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="notes-title">
      <div class="section-head">
        <h2 id="notes-title">Latest lab notes</h2>
        <p class="muted">Longer write-ups, posted when a study closes. They double as drafts of our thesis chapters.</p>
      </div>
      {posts.length > 0 ? (
        <ul class="note-list">
          {posts.map((p) => (
            <li>
              <a class="study-link" href={postPath(p)}>{p.data.title}</a>
              <p class="muted small" style="margin:2px 0 0">{fmtDate(p.data.date)} · {p.data.authors.join(", ")} · {p.data.workstream}</p>
            </li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      ) : (
        <p class="empty">
          The first write-ups are being edited now. Until then, every finished study has its own page with the verdict,
          the evidence and our reasoning. Start with the <a href="#studies">study table</a> above.
        </p>
      )}
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>
